00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.160
Let us start today’s episode by getting&nbsp;
to know our master of microscopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.868
James a little more…up close and personal.

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:49.818
Your screen is currently awash in James’ blood.

00:00:49.818 --> 00:00:55.821
Fortunately, this was a planned affair.&nbsp;
James didn’t accidentally cut himself on a slide and bleed all over it.

00:00:55.821 --> 00:01:00.960
 He simply placed a drop&nbsp;
on the slide, and let the cells do the rest. 

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.880
And so in observing his blood under&nbsp;
the microscope, James is joining a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:10.800
long tradition of scientists who have examined&nbsp;
blood belonging to themselves, to other animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.240
and to other organisms, so that we can decipher&nbsp;
the role this fluid plays in keeping us alive.&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.040
It’s been hard for us as humans&nbsp;
to miss the fact that blood is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.600
important to our bodies. But understanding&nbsp;
the nature and mechanisms of it has required&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.280
untangling the macroscopic structures that&nbsp;
send blood coursing through our veins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.520
and the microscopic details&nbsp;
that make blood what it is.&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.560
One of our early methods of describing and&nbsp;
categorizing the body were the four humors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.320
a system developed by ancient Greek&nbsp;
physicians who believed our body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:00.480
could be described as the interplay between&nbsp;
yellow bile, black bile, phlegm, and blood.&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.360
But blood is more than just the substance itself.&nbsp;
The fluid is nothing without a way to use it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.640
And so our understanding of blood is tied&nbsp;
up in our understanding of the whole system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.320
the circulatory system.
To get to what that means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.240
let’s start by looking at the blood of&nbsp;
something a bit different from us: daphnia.&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.320
One thing that makes daphnia handy for our&nbsp;
purposes is that you can, you know, literally see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:37.680
right through them. And with a microscope, you&nbsp;
can observe the rapid beating of its heart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.040
the flow of blood cells through its body.
Daphnia are also an invertebrate, and like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.040
many other invertebrates, it passes cells and ions&nbsp;
and other important molecules throughout its body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:56.080
using a watery fluid called hemolymph. Their&nbsp;
hemolymph isn’t constrained to veins or arteries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.160
Instead, it’s pumped into an&nbsp;
open cavity called the hemocoel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.560
where it then comes into contact with the&nbsp;
organs it needs to deliver nutrients to.&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.640
This is an open circulatory system. Vertebrates&nbsp;
like us, on the other hand, use a closed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.440
circulatory system where blood is directed&nbsp;
throughout our body in a contained network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.200
of blood vessels. That system allows for more&nbsp;
efficiency in delivering oxygen throughout our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:27.040
body, but it’s also more complex and requires more&nbsp;
energy compared to the open circulatory system.&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:34.640
Now, it took us quite a while to piece together&nbsp;
what our circulatory system would look like. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.480
second century physician Galen theorized that&nbsp;
blood is made in our livers using the remnants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:46.720
of digested food. And when blood arrived at&nbsp;
whatever spot it was needed, it was then consumed.&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.800
Galen’s hypothesized path mistook the nature&nbsp;
of blood and the structure of the heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.960
and yet it was a theory that would&nbsp;
prevail in medicine for centuries.&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.440
In the 13th century, the&nbsp;
Arab physician Ibn-al-Nafis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.360
drew on his study of animals to correctly&nbsp;
describe how our circulatory system works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:15.120
And yet his revolutionary work was not widely&nbsp;
known until 1924, when an Egyptian physician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.000
found a copy of it in the Berlin State&nbsp;
Library while working on his dissertation.&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.760
In the 17th century, English physician William&nbsp;
Harvey came to draw the same picture of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.360
circulatory system, turning to demonstrations&nbsp;
on dissected animals to gruesomely illustrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.440
his point. And his work would, in turn, go&nbsp;
on to change our understanding of blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.240
emphasizing that it is a thing made and&nbsp;
circulated by our bodies, and not--as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.480
Galen predicted--a product of our digestion.
So what are some of the pieces that make up our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:56.160
blood? It’s complex and variable, of course, but&nbsp;
let’s focus on the color. For many invertebrates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.720
the hemolymph is either clear or tinted&nbsp;
yellow with pigment molecules. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.760
might be thinking right now, “ I’ve squashed&nbsp;
a fly before! And there was a lot red!”&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.280
And yes, perhaps there was red. But&nbsp;
that was actually due to the pigments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.360
in the insect’s eyes, not the blood.
But biology does love an exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.720
which takes us back to the Daphnia, as well&nbsp;
as to another invertebrate called Artemia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:29.040
Both of these invertebrate’s blood contains&nbsp;
a protein that is familiar to us: hemoglobin.&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.200
Hemoglobin is the four-part protein inside our&nbsp;
red blood cells that makes them red. And more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:44.480
importantly, hemoglobin attaches to oxygen. When&nbsp;
a red blood cell gets to our lungs, the hemoglobin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:51.680
binds to oxygen, turning the red blood cell&nbsp;
into our own personal oxygen transport machine.&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.520
Now there are other oxygen-binding molecules used&nbsp;
by other animals, but Daphnia and Artemia both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:03.040
turn to hemoglobin when times get tough. When&nbsp;
the waters around them get low in oxygen, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:10.080
adapt by producing more and more hemoglobin so&nbsp;
they can capture more of that precious resource.&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.920
As they produce more hemoglobin, the&nbsp;
populations of Daphnia and Artemia become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:22.720
visibly redder—like they’re blushing—which might&nbsp;
explain why they don’t just have hemoglobin lying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.600
around in large quantities all the time. Not&nbsp;
only does it take energy to make hemoglobin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:34.240
it makes the animals more visible to predators.
When you look at James’ blood cells, you can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.680
the red of the hemoglobin. But as we said&nbsp;
at the start of this episode, James is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.960
of a long line of scientists who have examined&nbsp;
their blood under the microscope. Some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:52.400
early microscopic studies of human blood relied on&nbsp;
samples taken from clots or a recently fed louse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.480
James has actually made a note on the script&nbsp;
here. It says, “Glad I didn’t have to do this.”&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.080
But of course one of the early adopters&nbsp;
of the self-study approach was none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.800
than the early master of microscopes,&nbsp;
Antoni Van Leeuwenhoek, who described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.720
the “sanguineous globules” of his own blood&nbsp;
in a letter to the Royal Society of London.&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.440
Centuries later, Dr. Karl Landsteiner would&nbsp;
collect samples from six of his co-workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:25.600
and himself to study how these different blood&nbsp;
samples and the antibodies within them reacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:31.920
to each other. These observations would lead him&nbsp;
to describe the human ABO blood grouping system.&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.160
We have always known that our blood was vital,&nbsp;
in both senses of that word. And though we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.840
now teased apart much of its true nature,&nbsp;
it’s significance reaches beyond scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:50.800
understanding. We talk of blood relations,&nbsp;
and of being hot blooded or red blooded. Blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:58.400
is always available to us, both for science&nbsp;
and symbolism, for medicine and metaphor.&nbsp;

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.920
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

